  [38;5;153msubmit → validateForm → login → (stub auth) → trackEvent → return {success, user, userId}[39m

  The existing submit handler needs no change; it already logs the whole result, which now carries [38;5;153muserId[39m.

  [1m5. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mtrackEvent[39m must never break login. I'll wrap the call in a [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m that swallows and console-warns on failure —
  tracking is diagnostic, and a broken analytics call should not fail an authentication that otherwise succeeded. This
  matters more once a real network POST is behind the seam.

  [1m6. Testing — and a tooling question[0m

  There is no test infrastructure: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no dependencies, no runner, and nothing in the repo has
  ever been tested. So "add tests" here means "set up testing," which is a real choice rather than a detail.

  Cheapest to decide now, before code exists. My recommendation is the first option only — Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m
  needs zero dependencies and would cover [38;5;153mtrackEvent[39m and [38;5;153mlogin[39m if I extract them to be importable under both Node and
  the browser. Linting and e2e are likely overkill for a fixture this size, but they're cheapest to add now if you want
  them.
